 ## Kha-Ba-La as Maintenance Loop
 
 Kha is the field that recognizes accumulation. It is not a deity. It is the combined sensory apparatus of a calendar, a priesthood, and a collective that has learned to read its own stress signatures. In the Padyami, kha is triggered not by catastrophe but by date. The calendar says: now. The field looks, finds the load, acknowledges it. This is the recognition function. Without it, the system is blind until crash.
 
-Ba is the vessel that receives the load. It is the effigy, the offerings, the procession itself as a moving body. But Ba is also the body of every participant who wakes at 3 AM, shoulders a basket, walks in silence with feet that will blister by dawn. The vessel is distributed. The pointer is collective. This is why the offering is inert material rather than a human being: they have learned, over centuries, to externalize the pointer — to make it object rather than subject — so that the sweep does not require blood. The Greeks never solved this. The Indian tradition did, by making the vessel biodegradable.
